1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:00,870
<u>Computer Science</u>

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:03,332
<i>Name the principles of OOP</i>

3
00:00:04,570 --> 00:00:07,550
Encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, and abstraction.

4
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:12,930
They help separate responsibilities, hide implementation details, and work with objects through common contracts.

5
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:17,700
Encapsulation means hiding internal state and implementation details behind a public API.

6
00:00:18,410 --> 00:00:22,460
For example, a field can be private, while access to it is controlled through methods or properties.

7
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:26,470
Polymorphism allows us to work with different implementations through a common type.

8
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:32,410
For example, a ViewModel can depend on a Repository interface, while a concrete implementation is provided through DI.

9
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:36,990
Inheritance allows a new class to be created from an existing one and reuse its behavior.

10
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:42,050
Deep inheritance hierarchies increase coupling, so composition is often preferred in application code.

11
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:47,580
Abstraction keeps the important properties and behavior visible while hiding unnecessary implementation details.

12
00:00:48,290 --> 00:00:53,060
In Kotlin and Java, it is usually expressed through interfaces, abstract classes, and public contracts.

13
00:00:54,561 --> 00:00:56,171
<i>What are mutability and immutability?</i>

14
00:00:57,689 --> 00:01:01,319
A mutable object can be changed after creation, while an immutable object cannot.

15
00:01:02,019 --> 00:01:06,399
Immutability makes state management, concurrency, and declarative UI easier to reason about.

16
00:01:08,257 --> 00:01:08,967
<i>What is SOLID?</i>

17
00:01:10,242 --> 00:01:15,692
SOLID is a set of five object-oriented design principles: SRP, OCP, LSP, ISP, and DIP.

18
00:01:16,392 --> 00:01:19,992
Their goal is to reduce coupling and make code easier to extend, test, and maintain.

19
00:01:20,702 --> 00:01:25,342
Single Responsibility Principle means that a class should have one main responsibility and one reason to change.

20
00:01:26,042 --> 00:01:31,282
For example, a ViewModel should not handle networking, database access, formatting, and navigation at the same time.

21
00:01:31,992 --> 00:01:36,992
Open/Closed Principle means that software entities should be open for extension but closed for constant modification.

22
00:01:37,702 --> 00:01:42,812
New behavior is better added through composition, strategies, and new implementations rather than growing if or when blocks.

23
00:01:43,512 --> 00:01:47,102
Liskov Substitution Principle means that a subtype must be able to replace its base type

24
00:01:47,122 --> 00:01:49,282
without breaking its contract or expected behavior.

25
00:01:49,992 --> 00:01:55,162
Interface Segregation Principle means that several small, focused interfaces are usually better than one large interface.

26
00:01:55,872 --> 00:01:58,212
A client should not depend on methods it does not use.

27
00:01:58,912 --> 00:02:03,292
Dependency Inversion Principle means that high-level logic should not depend directly on low-level details.

28
00:02:04,002 --> 00:02:08,292
Both should depend on abstractions, for example a ViewModel depending on a Repository interface.

29
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:11,970
<i>What practices exist besides SOLID?</i>

30
00:02:13,234 --> 00:02:19,424
Examples include DRY, KISS, YAGNI, separation of concerns, composition over inheritance, immutability, dependency injection,

31
00:02:19,564 --> 00:02:21,074
code review, and automated testing.

32
00:02:21,774 --> 00:02:27,184
These are guidelines rather than absolute rules, and they should be applied based on context to reduce complexity and the cost of change.

33
00:02:28,689 --> 00:02:29,669
<i>What is a code smell?</i>

34
00:02:31,174 --> 00:02:34,564
A code smell is a sign of a possible design problem, not necessarily a bug.

35
00:02:35,274 --> 00:02:40,354
Examples include a very large class, a long method, duplicated code, too many parameters, and strong coupling.

36
00:02:41,881 --> 00:02:44,031
<i>What are the main rules for designing a code API?</i>

37
00:02:45,571 --> 00:02:48,971
An API should be minimal, clear, consistent, and difficult to misuse.

38
00:02:49,681 --> 00:02:53,731
It should hide implementation details, use clear names and types, preserve backward compatibility,

39
00:02:53,881 --> 00:02:56,011
and avoid exposing mutable state without a good reason.

40
00:02:57,550 --> 00:02:58,320
<i>What is Big O?</i>

41
00:02:59,843 --> 00:03:04,293
Big O describes how an algorithm's execution time or memory usage grows as the input size increases.

42
00:03:05,003 --> 00:03:08,033
It is a scalability estimate, not an exact time in milliseconds.

43
00:03:09,537 --> 00:03:11,327
<i>What is the complexity of binary search?</i>

44
00:03:12,838 --> 00:03:16,908
Binary search takes O(log n) on sorted data with O(1) access to the middle element,

45
00:03:17,118 --> 00:03:18,388
halving the search range at each step.

46
00:03:20,281 --> 00:03:22,141
<i>What is the average complexity of QuickSort?</i>

47
00:03:23,394 --> 00:03:27,654
On average, QuickSort works in O(n log n), while the worst case is O(n²).

48
00:03:28,354 --> 00:03:32,024
In practice, good implementations use safer pivot selection and hybrid approaches.

49
00:03:33,550 --> 00:03:34,680
<i>ArrayList or LinkedList?</i>

50
00:03:36,203 --> 00:03:40,833
ArrayList provides O(1) indexed access and usually performs better in practice because of cache locality.

51
00:03:41,543 --> 00:03:47,163
LinkedList is useful less often: indexed lookup is O(n), and fast insertion only helps when the target node is already known.

52
00:03:48,681 --> 00:03:51,381
<i>What must be overridden when a class is used as a HashMap key?</i>

53
00:03:52,899 --> 00:03:56,319
Override equals() and hashCode() together when custom value equality is needed;

54
00:03:56,519 --> 00:03:58,429
inherited identity equality is valid too.

55
00:03:59,139 --> 00:04:02,539
If two objects are equal according to equals(), they must return the same hash code.

56
00:04:04,062 --> 00:04:05,372
<i>What data structures do you know?</i>

57
00:04:06,902 --> 00:04:12,452
Common data structures include arrays, dynamic arrays, linked lists, stacks, queues, deques, hash tables, sets, trees,

58
00:04:12,582 --> 00:04:13,772
heaps, tries, and graphs.

59
00:04:15,305 --> 00:04:18,255
<i>What is the worst-case insertion complexity in a binary search tree?</i>

60
00:04:19,763 --> 00:04:24,693
In an unbalanced binary search tree, insertion can degrade to O(n) if the tree becomes similar to a linked list.

61
00:04:25,403 --> 00:04:29,063
In a balanced tree, such as an AVL or red-black tree, insertion is O(log n).

62
00:04:30,926 --> 00:04:32,856
<i>What is the lookup complexity in a HashMap?</i>

63
00:04:34,130 --> 00:04:37,050
Average lookup is O(1) when hash codes are distributed well.

64
00:04:37,740 --> 00:04:40,180
In the worst case, many collisions can degrade it to O(n),

65
00:04:40,390 --> 00:04:44,440
while modern Java implementations may use a treeified bucket and provide O(log n) lookup there.

66
00:04:45,961 --> 00:04:49,361
<i>What happens if several threads modify a regular HashMap at the same time?</i>

67
00:04:50,875 --> 00:04:55,885
HashMap is not thread-safe: this can cause lost updates, visibility problems, and an inconsistent internal state.

68
00:04:56,585 --> 00:04:58,555
The solution is to confine access to one thread,

69
00:04:58,755 --> 00:05:01,555
protect operations with the same lock, or use a concurrent collection.

70
00:05:03,070 --> 00:05:04,530
<i>How does ConcurrentHashMap work?</i>

71
00:05:06,059 --> 00:05:09,569
ConcurrentHashMap provides thread-safe access without one global lock for the whole map.

72
00:05:10,279 --> 00:05:14,999
Reads are mostly non-blocking, while updates use atomic operations and local synchronization for individual buckets;

73
00:05:15,219 --> 00:05:18,649
compound operations should use methods such as putIfAbsent(), compute(), or merge().

74
00:05:20,169 --> 00:05:21,209
<i>What are stack and heap?</i>

75
00:05:22,726 --> 00:05:26,586
The stack stores call frames, local variables, and references, while the heap stores objects.

76
00:05:27,276 --> 00:05:30,926
Each thread has its own stack, while the heap is shared and managed by the Garbage Collector.

77
00:05:32,449 --> 00:05:33,439
<i>What is a memory leak?</i>

78
00:05:34,963 --> 00:05:39,003
A memory leak happens when an object is no longer needed but is still reachable through a chain of references,

79
00:05:39,183 --> 00:05:40,443
so the GC cannot remove it.

80
00:05:41,153 --> 00:05:45,890
Common Android causes include references to an Activity, listeners, callbacks, and long-lived singleton objects.
